<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Indian Ocean World</h1><p class="path-tagline">Monsoon winds, ivory, gold and pepper: the sea that joined East Africa, Arabia, India and the Indies.</p><p class="path-intro">Long before Europeans sailed into it, the Indian Ocean was one of the great highways of the world's trade. Its monsoon winds blew one way in summer and the other in winter, and merchants timed their voyages to them, carrying ivory and gold from Africa, pepper from Malabar, and silk and porcelain from China. This path follows the ports and powers that grew rich on that traffic, from Aksum and Srivijaya to the Swahili sultans of Kilwa and the Zamorins of Calicut, and ends as Portuguese guns arrived to claim the trade.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Navigator</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monsoon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Monsoon</span><span class="path-step-why">The seasonal reversal of winds that governed farming and seaborne trade across the region: sailors timed their voyages to it.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Work · mid-1st century CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</span><span class="path-step-why">A Greek guide of the mid-1st century CE to the ports and goods from Egypt to East Africa and India, with the earliest historical mention of Aksum.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/aksumite-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 100 CE – 940 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Aksumite Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Grew rich sending African ivory and gold out through its Red Sea port of Adulis, and minted its own gold coins.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/srivijaya-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 650 CE – 1377 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Srivijaya Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">From Palembang on Sumatra it ruled the Strait of Malacca from the 7th to the 13th century, taxing the shipping between China and India.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/chola-invasion-of-srivijaya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chola-invasion-of-srivijaya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1025; 1068</span><span class="path-step-name">Chola invasion of Srivijaya</span><span class="path-step-why">In 1025 a Chola fleet from southern India sacked Srivijaya's ports and seized its ruler, breaking the monopoly it lived on.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kilwa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 957 CE – 1513 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kilwa Sultanate</span><span class="path-step-why">An island sultanate that grew rich as the middleman for the gold of the Zimbabwe plateau; Ibn Battuta praised its sultan around 1331.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/ming-treasure-voyages"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ming-treasure-voyages.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1405–1433</span><span class="path-step-name">Ming treasure voyages</span><span class="path-step-why">Seven great fleets under Zheng He reached Arabia and East Africa between 1405 and 1433; then the voyages were ended and never resumed.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zamorin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Zamorin</span><span class="path-step-why">Calicut's rulers grew rich on the spice trade with Arab and other Muslim merchants, received Vasco da Gama in 1498 and then fought the Portuguese.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/malacca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malacca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Malacca</span><span class="path-step-why">Under its sultanate, founded around 1400, it became South-East Asia's leading entrepôt, until the Portuguese captured it in 1511.</span></span></a></li></ol>
